title IIIF_Auth

actor User
participant ClientViewer
participant MessageFrame
participant Publisher

note over ClientViewer, MessageFrame:
    These two participants are both in the
    same browser; the MessageFrame is 
    created by the client viewer app.
end note

User -> ClientViewer: Action that requires image service\n(e.g., "view deep zoom image")
activate ClientViewer
ClientViewer -> Publisher: (XHR) GET image/info.json
note right of Publisher:
    This image service requires auth,
    and the GET request did not include
    a bearer token. We still serve the same 
    JSON body (no secrets in the *description*)
    but with a 401 status code, to indicate that
    the client can't *use* the service.
end note
Publisher->Publisher: HTTP 401 Unauthorised
note over ClientViewer:
    The 401 response for the service description tells the 
    client viewer app that if initiates requests for *content*
    from the image service (e.g., tiles for deep zoom, 
    crops, thumbnails), then these requests will *FAIL*.
    The client needs to do something about this.
end note
ClientViewer -> ClientViewer: info.json has\nlogin_service?
note left of ClientViewer
    (assume it does, in this flow)
end note
note right of ClientViewer
    We need to get the User to do something at Publisher
    that will turn the HTTP 401 into a 200.
    We don't know or care *what* they do there!
end note 
ClientViewer -> Publisher: publisherWindow = window.open(login_service)
ClientViewer->ClientViewer: begin to poll for\npublisherWindow.closed
note right of User:
    User sees the publisher's login
    page open in a new tab
end note
User -> Publisher: User authenticates
activate Publisher
note right of ClientViewer
    The client viewer app has no visibility of
    what happens here, and doesn't care
    what "User authenticates" means.
end note
note right of Publisher:
    Here the flow can do anything, e.g., enter
    an OAuth2 flow, social login, campus SSO,
    or any other interpretation of "authenticate",
    which might just be agreeing to terms.
end note
note left of ClientViewer:
    (still polling...)
end note
Publisher -> Publisher: set_cookie()
Publisher -> Publisher: serve a page with\nwindow.close()
deactivate Publisher
ClientViewer->ClientViewer: detect publisherWindow.closed\nstop polling
ClientViewer->MessageFrame: set iframe src to token_service
activate MessageFrame
deactivate ClientViewer
MessageFrame->Publisher: (simple) GET token_service\nWILL HAVE PUBLISHER'S COOKIE
activate Publisher
Publisher->Publisher: Get token for cookie
Publisher->Publisher: Build messaging JavaScript
deactivate Publisher
MessageFrame->ClientViewer: postMessage(token)
deactivate MessageFrame
activate ClientViewer
ClientViewer -> Publisher: GET image/info.json\nWITH BEARER TOKEN token
note right of Publisher:
    This time, the server sees the bearer token in the
    request header. It matches the token to the previously
    granted cookie (the token is not the same as the cookie).
    It returns the same info.json but this time with a 200
    status code, to indicate that the client CAN use the service.
end note
note over ClientViewer:
    The 200 response for the service description tells the 
    client viewer app that if initiates requests for *content*
    from the image service (e.g., tiles for deep zoom, 
    crops, thumbnails), then these requests will *SUCCEED*
end note
ClientViewer->Publisher: (e.g., image requests...)
note left of ClientViewer:
   The client app can now create whatever user
   experience it wants to from the image service.
end note




